You are right and I was wrong. Your fixtures were captured, mine came from the API reference, and captured payloads win.

I checked the page you linked. Its Webhooks 2.0 example is:

```json
{"type":"activity.sent","created_at":"2025-08-05T21:23:54.000000Z",
 "data":{"id":"6892766a5b66e2daf3dc9155","account_id":1,"domain_id":1,
         "message_id":"6892766ae78995a317577aa1","email_id":"6892766a8d52ba62543d5e71",
         "type":"sent","subject":"Qui consequatur hic sit quas.",
         "recipient":"fisher.harley@example.net","tags":["test","test2"],"meta":null}}
```

So MailerSend publishes two examples of the same event, with the same `created_at` down to the microsecond, that disagree on three points: `recipient` against `email`, `account_id` present against absent, and `meta: null` against `meta: []`. Two documented shapes and your captures make it two to one for `recipient`, so my change would have broken the payloads you actually receive. Sorry for the detour, and thank you for pushing back with the evidence instead of taking my word for it.

I have pushed a fix. Rather than pick a side, the branch now accepts both:

```php
return $payload['data']['email']['recipient']['email'] ?? $payload['data']['recipient'] ?? $payload['data']['email'];
```

The three keys cannot collide. In version 1 `data.email` is an object, in version 2 it is a string, and `??` uses isset semantics, so a string offset lookup with a non-numeric key returns false and falls through instead of erroring. The parser guard now accepts either key as well, and I split it into named `$isV1` and `$isV2` locals so it stays readable.

Your fixtures are restored to `recipient` as you captured them, and I added `sent_email_key.json` covering the API reference shape, including `account_id` and `meta: null`, so both layouts are locked in by tests. I verified all four shapes end to end through the parser with a valid signature:

```
OK   whats-new v2 (recipient)   id=6892766ae78995a317577aa1 recipient=fisher.harley@example.net
OK   api-reference v2 (email)   id=6892766ae78995a317577aa1 recipient=test@mailersend.com
OK   v1 (nested email object)   id=5fc0d003f718c90162341852 recipient=v1@mailersend.com
OK   v2 click with meta         id=mid recipient=click@example.net meta={"ip":"10.0.0.1","url":"..."}
```

The bridge suite is green at 49 tests.

This also lowers the stakes on your open question. Since both key names are accepted, a real capture that disagrees with one of the documented examples can no longer break the recipient. Real payloads for a bounce, an open, a click and an unsubscribe are still worth having, since the `meta` keys are the last inferred part, but they are no longer blocking.

Do ask MailerSend to fix whichever page is wrong. Worth mentioning that the API reference and the release note contradict each other on the same example, since that is the part they can act on.
